        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The International Space Station (ISS) Expedition Two crew consisted of Expedition Commander Yury Usachev and Flight Engineers James Voss and Susan Helms. The crew launched on March 8, 2001, aboard the STS-102 Space Shuttle Discovery. Upon arrival, the station consisted of four modules: the Russian Zvezda Service Module, which served as living quarters and an on-board control center for the early station; the Zarya Control Module, a module that provides supplementary power and propulsion functions; the Unity Node (also called Node 1), which is a connecting module that provides the attachment points for future U.S. segments; and the U.S. Laboratory Module Destiny, the station's first science facility.
<P>
Four Shuttle missions supported this four-month mission. STS-102/5A.1 transported the Expedition Two crew and the Leonardo Multipurpose Logistics Module containing equipment racks for the Destiny Module to the station and returned the Expedition One crew to Earth. Leonardo was built by the Italian Space Agency (ASI) and is the first of three pressurized modules to serve as the International Space Station's "moving vans," carrying laboratory racks filled with equipment, experiments, and supplies to and from the station aboard the Space Shuttle. STS-100/6A delivered the ultra high frequency antenna, the Space Station Remote Manipulator System, and the Raffaello Multipurpose Logistics Module, which carried six system racks and two storage racks for the U.S. Laboratory. STS-104/7A brought the joint airlock and high-pressure gas assembly to support ISS spacewalk operations, and STS-105/7A.1 returned the Expedition Two crew to Earth and brought the third ISS crew to the station with the Donatello Multipurpose Logistics Module.
<P>
During the docked phase of the STS-102/5A.1 mission, two spacewalks were planned to prepare Leonardo for transfer from the Shuttle to the station to install hardware needed for the STS-100/6A mission and to deliver spare parts to ISS. The first spacewalk tasks included unplugging Portable Mating Adapter 3 from Node 1 electrical cables, Lab Cradle Assembly installation, and rigid umbilical installation. Between spacewalks, the Shuttle crew docked Leonardo to Node 1. During the second spacewalk, the crew transferred the Early Ammonia Servicer to the P6 truss, installed the External Stowage Platform, and delivered a replacement Pump Flow Control System. After the second spacewalk, the Shuttle's robot arm placed Leonardo back in the payload bay.
<P>
The crew planned to conduct at least two tagups each day with U.S. flight controllers using the slow-scan Internet-style video capability associated with the on-board early communications system. The U.S. KU-band communications equipment housed in the Z1 Truss structure, which was installed on the ISS during the STS-92 mission, was activated during Expedition Two.
<P>
Research on the International Space Station encompassed six research areas, including life sciences, microgravity, space science, Earth science, space product development, and engineering research and technology. Space-based research is supported by ground-based research. 
<P>
The Human Research Facility (HRF) is the first life sciences research facility on board the ISS. Biological research equipment to be used on the station includes the following: Habitat Holding Rack; Life Sciences Glovebox; Cell Culture Unit; Insect, Advanced Animal, and Aquatic Habitats; Plant Research Unit; Egg Incubator; and Centrifuge Accommodations Module. 
<P>
During Expedition Two, the crew worked with 18 different experiments. The research included studies of the human body in space, space radiation, observations of the Earth, crystal growth in weightlessness, and plant growth in space.
<P>
Life sciences experiments and payloads that began during Expedition Two were:
<P>
<UL>
<LI><B>Bonner Ball Neutron Detector</B> to develop safety measures against neutron radiation to protect crewmembers during long-duration space flights.</LI>
<LI><B>Crewmember and Crew-Ground Interactions During ISS Missions (Interactions)</B> to identify and characterize interpersonal and cultural factors that may affect crew and ground support personnel performance during space station missions.</LI>
<LI><B>Dosmap (Dosimetric Mapping)</B> attempts to record and map the different types of radiation that get inside the space station to help scientists more accurately predict the radiation exposure crewmembers will experience in space and to develop countermeasures to prolonged human exposure.</LI>
<LI><B>Effects of Altered Gravity on Spinal Cord Excitability (H-Reflex)</B> to study changes in neurovestibular function with the goal of determining if exercise could be made more effective during long-term space flight.</LI>
<LI><B>Phantom Torso</B> will help scientists more accurately predict the radiation exposure astronauts will experience inside their bodies - especially to critical blood-forming organs - and will learn how long human beings can remain in space before dangerous levels of radiation are absorbed by the body. The experiment may lead to countermeasures to safely prolong human exposure to radiation.</LI>
<LI><B>Sub-Regional Assessment of Bone Loss In the Axial Skeleton In Long-Term Space Flight</B> to measure bone loss and recovery for ISS crewmembers.</LI>
</UL>
<P>
The ISS serves as a virtual classroom in space. Live educational events and programs, such as EarthKAM (where students operate a camera on board the ISS to investigate changes on Earth), will motivate students to understand their world. 
<P>
The Expedition Two crew returned to Earth on August 22, 2001, on board the Space Shuttle Discovery at the completion of the STS-105 mission.
